    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
    "body": "Just to clarify, this is the current expected semantics of Triton, which behaves like C for which the following also overflows:\r\n```\r\n  int32_t x = 1 << 31;\r\n  int32_t y = (1 << 31) + 3;\r\n  auto z = x * y;\r\n  std::cout << z << std::endl;\r\n```\r\nThis is however definitely surprising behavior for a python-based DSL, but we haven't found a good way to address this yet. If `mul` returns int64, then everything becomes extremely slow. I see two ways of dealing with this issue, both of which seem so cumbersome I'm not sure we want them:\r\n\r\n- Extreme JIT specialization in the compiler. All int arguments of the kernel are specialized for the number of bits they fit in. Then the compiler can propagate this information and allocate the right amount bits. I fear this will lead to just way too much kernel specialization and make AOT basically intractable.\r\n- Warnings. Whenever a user multiplies to int32 together, we emit a warning that asks to either explicitly cast to int64 or assert that it's safe to keep the result in int64. This will lead to very cumbersome kernels.\r\n\r\nObviously, a third way is just to document this behavior better without modifying it.\r\n\r\nI'm open to suggestions.",
