    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "`int64_t` indexing in cuda kernels is slower, but not dramatically so, ~15% penalty I'd say. Also, there are no multiplications here, only divisions, and that works in c++:\r\n```\r\n  uint32_t xindex = 3288334335;\r\n  auto x1 = (xindex / 12544) % 64;\r\n  std::cout << xindex << \" \" << x1  << \" \" << xindex/12544 << \"\\n\";\r\n```\r\n`xnumel` is correctly annotated as u32, but division result isn't, and I don't think it's expected even in C. \r\nIn any case, someone will try gigantic tensor with more than even uint_max number of elements and we have to have a plan for that. We can annotate the size arguments with int64_t or int32_t with the corresponding guards. I thought I tried that and it didn't help, but I'll double check. For us, it won't lead to explosion, because we only have 2 size arguments. \r\nSo, if annotation with i64 works, we can work around this at the expense of some perf penalty, if it doesn't, I'll get back to you :-)",
